### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants filter-then-map: filter integers above a threshold, then convert to strings. This tests operation order, the filter-then-map pattern, and null handling.

### How do you filter and convert?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class FilterThenMap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> amounts = List.of(
            25000, 50000, 75000, 100000, 150000, 200000);
        int threshold = 50000;

        List<String> highValue = amounts.stream()
            .filter(n -> n > threshold)
            .map(String::valueOf)
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Above Rs." + threshold + ": " + highValue);
    }
}
```

```text
Above Rs.50000: [75000, 100000, 150000, 200000]
```

### Why filter before map?

```java
// GOOD: filter first, map only survivors
amounts.stream()
    .filter(n -> n > threshold)  // shrinks the stream
    .map(String::valueOf)        // converts only what passed
    .toList();

// BAD: map everything, then filter on strings
amounts.stream()
    .map(String::valueOf)        // converts ALL elements
    .filter(s -> Integer.parseInt(s) > threshold)  // parses back!
    .toList();
```

Filtering first avoids unnecessary work. Place the cheap, selective operation before the expensive transformation.

### How do you handle nulls in the list?

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Objects;

List<Integer> withNulls = Arrays.asList(25000, null, 75000, null);

// WRONG: NullPointerException when unboxing null to int
// withNulls.stream().filter(n -> n > 50000)...

// CORRECT: filter nulls first
List<String> safe = withNulls.stream()
    .filter(Objects::nonNull)
    .filter(n -> n > 50000)
    .map(String::valueOf)
    .toList();
```

### How do you format the output as currency?

```java
import java.text.NumberFormat;
import java.util.Locale;

NumberFormat inr = NumberFormat.getCurrencyInstance(
    new Locale("en", "IN"));

List<String> formatted = amounts.stream()
    .filter(n -> n > threshold)
    .map(n -> inr.format(n))
    .toList();

System.out.println(formatted);
```

### How is filter-then-map different from map-then-filter?

| Order | Behavior | Efficiency |
|---|---|---|
| filter then map | Converts only retained elements | Better |
| map then filter | Converts all, then discards some | Wasteful |

Always place the more selective operation first in the pipeline.

### What about using mapToInt for primitive operations?

```java
// If you need the count of values above threshold
long count = amounts.stream()
    .mapToInt(Integer::intValue)
    .filter(n -> n > threshold)
    .count();
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using == instead of > for the threshold.

```java
// WRONG: keeps only values EQUAL to threshold
.filter(n -> n == threshold)

// CORRECT: keeps values ABOVE threshold
.filter(n -> n > threshold)
```

Mistake: comparing boxed Integers with == instead of equals.

```java
// WRONG for large values: == compares references
Integer a = 1000;
Integer b = 1000;
System.out.println(a == b);      // false (different objects)
System.out.println(a.equals(b)); // true

// For primitives in filter lambdas, unboxing handles this correctly:
// n -> n > threshold unboxes both sides
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you convert to a comma-separated string?" Chain .collect(Collectors.joining(", ")).
- "How do you map to a different format?" Use map with a formatting lambda.
- "How do you collect both the passing and failing elements?" Use partitioningBy.
